1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,817
Turning a lab bench into an industry fell to a different

2
00:00:03,817 --> 00:00:05,454
man entirely: Carl Bosch,

3
00:00:05,454 --> 00:00:08,597
an engineer at the German chemical company BASF.

4
00:00:08,597 --> 00:00:10,954
Bosch didn't discover the reaction —

5
00:00:10,954 --> 00:00:14,031
he had to make it survive contact with reality.

6
00:00:14,031 --> 00:00:16,847
He needed cheap hydrogen at massive volume.

7
00:00:16,847 --> 00:00:20,317
He needed a catalyst that wouldn't fail within hours,

8
00:00:20,317 --> 00:00:24,050
since Haber's rare osmium and uranium could never scale —

9
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,633
a search that took his colleague Alwin Mittasch roughly 2,000 separate

10
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:31,514
experiments before they landed on a cheaper,

11
00:00:31,514 --> 00:00:32,824
iron-based catalyst.

12
00:00:32,824 --> 00:00:37,997
And he needed steel vessels that could hold extreme pressure without cracking —

13
00:00:37,997 --> 00:00:44,806
an early problem that destroyed equipment through hydrogen embrittlement before his engineers solved it.

14
00:00:44,806 --> 00:00:49,259
In 1913, the world's first full-scale ammonia plant opened at Oppau,

15
00:00:49,259 --> 00:00:50,259
Germany.

16
00:00:50,259 --> 00:00:52,717
The air itself had become a raw material.

